<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    `26-34`: the adaptive-icon background layer. Full-bleed 108x108dp fill, no corner rounding -
    the brandbook's own 34px web tile uses `border-radius: 9px`, but that is a web-only detail
    that must NOT be baked in here: the *launcher* masks this layer into a circle, squircle,
    rounded-square or teardrop depending on the OEM, and a radius baked into the background would
    fight whatever shape the launcher applies on top of it
    (docs/backlog/26-34-the-app-has-no-launcher-icon.md).

    Gradient: the brandbook's own `linear-gradient(140deg, #4C8DFF, #9D6BFF)` (the same blue/violet
    swatch pair as the landing page), converted from the CSS angle to this vector's
    startX/Y-endX/Y coordinates:

      CSS gradient angle convention: 0deg = "to top", increasing clockwise. In a y-down coordinate
      system (this canvas's own, same as SVG/Android vector space) that makes the direction unit
      vector `(sin(theta), -cos(theta))`.

      theta = 140deg -> sin(140deg) = 0.642788, cos(140deg) = -0.766044
      direction = (0.642788, -(-0.766044)) = (0.642788, 0.766044)   // right-and-down, leaning
                                                                      // down (140 is 40deg short
                                                                      // of straight-down/180deg,
                                                                      // 50deg past right/90deg)

      Half-length of the gradient line on a WxH box (the same corner-covering formula the CSS
      gradient spec itself uses), W = H = 108:
        half_length = (108*|sin140deg| + 108*|cos140deg|) / 2
                    = (108*0.642788 + 108*0.766044) / 2
                    = 152.154 / 2 = 76.077

      center = (54, 54) (the 108x108 box's own center)
      start (0%, blue)   = center - half_length*direction = (54 - 48.901, 54 - 58.278)
                          = (5.099, -4.278)
      end   (100%, violet) = center + half_length*direction = (54 + 48.901, 54 + 58.278)
                          = (102.901, 112.278)

      The endpoints fall slightly outside the 0..108 box on purpose: this is the same formula CSS
      itself uses to guarantee the box's actual corners land ON the gradient line's segment
      (visible only at the exact 45deg-multiple angles do start/end coincide with box corners),
      not an approximation. Blue sits at the start (upper-left-ish, since the direction points
      down-and-right), violet at the end (lower-right-ish) - confirmed against the rendered
      drawable, see the commit report.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path android:pathData="M0,0H108V108H0Z">
        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
            <gradient
                android:type="linear"
                android:startX="5.099"
                android:startY="-4.278"
                android:endX="102.901"
                android:endY="112.278"
                android:startColor="#4C8DFF"
                android:endColor="#9D6BFF" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </path>
</vector>
